  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms impacted mainly western\nportions of the area this morning, with partly to mostly sunny\nskies returning to much of the local area this afternoon. As\nskies cleared, temperatures climbed, and most areas have reached\nthe mid to upper 80s over the past hour. For tonight, expect \nanother round of high plains convection to impact parts of the \nlocal area, focused mainly west of Highway 183. With each \nhourly model run of the CAMS pulling back the best chances for\nconvection to our west, confidence in storms is not \nparticularly high, and trimmed back pops significantly for this \nevening. That said, the ingredients remain in place for a few \nsevere storms tonight, although if realized, they will likely \nbe limited to our westernmost areas (where the slight risk for \nsevere weather remains from the SPC).\n\nBehind tonights potential convection, the upper level ridge to\nour west begins to become positively tilted as it expands across\nthe local area through the end of the week. This will result in\nmore spotty thunderstorm chances each day, mainly focused \nacross western and potentially northern portions of the area\nduring the evening and overnight hours each day. At the same \ntime, temperatures will be on the increase, peaking across the \nlocal area over the upcoming weekend. This could result in heat \nindex values once again climbing above 100 degrees in spots, \nespecially across portions of north central Kansas. At the same \ntime winds will be on the increase, and expect a breezy end to \nthe week and start to the weekend as the surface pressure \ngradient increases between low pressure on the lee side of the \nRockies and high pressure to our east. \n\nEventually the upper level ridge is expected to weaken and shift\neast late in the weekend or early next week, ushering in\nslightly more seasonable temperatures along with increasing\nchances for showers and thunderstorms as early as Sunday night,\nand then continuing off and on through much of next week. This\nwill be in response to a more progressive west to southwesterly\nflow pattern being realized across the Central Plains.",
